BEGIN;

INSERT INTO questions (section_id, topic, stem, choices, correct_index, explanation, difficulty, cognitive_level)
VALUES

-- Q1: easy / L1 — Scanning as analytical procedure technique
(1, 'Analytical Procedures', 'What is scanning as it relates to analytical procedures performed during an audit?', '["Reviewing accounting data to identify significant unusual items requiring further investigation", "Electronically copying all general ledger transactions into audit software", "Performing a detailed recalculation of every recorded journal entry", "Sending confirmation requests to all major customers and vendors"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Correct (A): AU-C 520.A1. Scanning is an analytical procedure in which the auditor reviews accounting data to identify significant or unusual items for further investigation, such as large or uncommon journal entries. It is a less precise but efficient technique used especially during final review. Wrong (B): Copying data into software is a data extraction step, not an analytical procedure itself. Wrong (C): Detailed recalculation of every entry is a test of details, not an analytical procedure. Wrong (D): Sending confirmations is a substantive test of details, not an analytical procedure.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q2: medium / L3 — Applying analytics to detect related-party transactions
(1, 'Analytical Procedures', 'During planning analytics for Granite Industries, the auditor notices that consulting expense increased 300% year-over-year while revenue remained flat, and all increases trace to a single vendor not previously used. Which conclusion should the auditor draw from this analytical procedure result?', '["The increase is immaterial because consulting expenses are typically a small line item", "The unusual pattern may indicate undisclosed related-party transactions requiring further investigation", "The auditor should immediately issue a qualified opinion for the misstatement", "No additional procedures are needed because the expense was properly recorded in the ledger"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct (B): AU-C 520.07. When analytical procedures reveal unusual fluctuations concentrated with a single counterparty, the auditor should consider whether undisclosed related-party transactions exist and perform targeted inquiry and investigation per AU-C 550. Wrong (A): Materiality is assessed in context; a 300% increase to one vendor is a red flag regardless of absolute size. Wrong (C): A qualified opinion is premature; the auditor must first investigate the cause of the fluctuation. Wrong (D): Proper recording does not address whether the transaction involves a related party or represents fraud.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q3: medium / L3 — Analytics and changes in accounting policy
(1, 'Analytical Procedures', 'Westfield Corp changed its revenue recognition method from completed-contract to percentage-of-completion during the current year. The auditor is performing substantive analytical procedures on revenue. How should the auditor address the change when developing the expectation?', '["Ignore the change since the new method is GAAP-compliant", "Use only the current year data to avoid comparability issues entirely", "Adjust the prior-year data to reflect the new accounting method before developing the expectation", "Reduce the threshold for acceptable difference to compensate for the policy change"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Correct (C): AU-C 520.A5. When accounting policies change, prior-period data must be adjusted to a comparable basis before using it to develop an expectation. Without this adjustment, differences caused by the policy change could be misinterpreted as misstatements. Wrong (A): Ignoring the change would create an incomparable baseline, undermining the analytical procedure''s effectiveness. Wrong (B): Discarding prior-year data eliminates trend analysis capability, reducing the precision of the expectation. Wrong (D): Reducing the threshold does not solve the comparability problem and may cause false positive investigations.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q4: hard / L4 — Evaluating conflicting analytical and detail evidence
(1, 'Analytical Procedures', 'During the audit of Pinnacle Manufacturing, the auditor''s substantive analytical procedure on cost of goods sold yields an expected amount within 2% of the recorded balance. However, tests of details on inventory pricing reveal systematic overstatement of standard costs by 8%. Which course of action best addresses this conflicting evidence?', '["Accept the analytical procedure result because the 2% difference is within the established threshold", "Reduce assessed control risk to justify the analytical procedure result", "Expand the scope of tests of details on other cost components unrelated to inventory", "Investigate why the analytical procedure failed to detect the pricing error and reassess the reliability of the data and assumptions used"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Correct (D): AU-C 520.07 and AU-C 500.11. When substantive analytical procedures contradict tests of details, the auditor must investigate the inconsistency. The analytical procedure may have used unreliable data or insufficiently precise assumptions that masked the inventory pricing misstatement. The auditor should reassess the procedure''s design rather than accept either result uncritically. Wrong (A): Accepting the analytical result ignores contradictory evidence from more precise detail testing. Wrong (B): Control risk cannot be retroactively reduced to resolve contradictory audit evidence. Wrong (C): Expanding testing on unrelated components does not resolve the identified pricing misstatement.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q5: hard / L4 — Evaluating appropriate level of disaggregation for multi-segment entity
(1, 'Analytical Procedures', 'An auditor is testing revenue for a client with three divisions: government contracting (40% of revenue, stable growth), commercial retail (35%, seasonal), and international consulting (25%, volatile). The auditor performs a single substantive analytical procedure using consolidated revenue data. Revenue in total is within 1% of the expectation, but two divisions individually differ by 12% and 15% in offsetting directions. What should the auditor conclude?', '["The consolidated procedure is insufficient because offsetting divisional differences mask potential misstatements, requiring disaggregated testing", "The analytical procedure provides sufficient evidence because the total difference falls within the acceptable threshold for the engagement", "The auditor should accept the consolidated result and note the offsetting differences in the workpapers for documentation", "The auditor should increase the threshold to account for the inherent variability among the three divisions"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Correct (A): AU-C 520.A13. Analytical procedures are more effective when applied to disaggregated data. When divisions have different characteristics and large offsetting variances exist, consolidated testing can mask material misstatements. The auditor must test at a level of disaggregation that prevents offsetting errors from going undetected. Wrong (B): A small total difference does not provide assurance when large offsetting variances exist at the component level. Wrong (C): Merely documenting the issue without further testing fails to address the risk of masked misstatements. Wrong (D): Increasing the threshold does not resolve the fundamental aggregation problem.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q6: medium / L3 — Applying analytics in a review engagement
(1, 'Analytical Procedures', 'During a review engagement for Redwood Services LLC, the accountant''s analytical procedures reveal that the current ratio declined from 2.1 to 0.8 year-over-year. Management states that a large loan was reclassified from long-term to current. How should the accountant respond?', '["Withdraw from the engagement because the decline indicates potential fraud", "Evaluate whether management''s explanation is consistent with the accountant''s understanding of the entity and, if plausible, consider whether additional procedures are needed", "Perform tests of details on the loan reclassification because the decline is material", "Issue an adverse conclusion on the financial statements due to the liquidity decline"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct (B): AR-C 90.24-.25. In a review engagement, the accountant obtains limited assurance primarily through analytical procedures and inquiry. When analytics reveal unusual items, the accountant evaluates management''s explanations for consistency and plausibility, performing additional analytical procedures or inquiries if needed. Wrong (A): Withdrawal is premature; a plausible explanation may exist and should be evaluated first. Wrong (C): Tests of details are audit-level procedures; a review relies on analytics and inquiry, not detailed testing. Wrong (D): An adverse conclusion requires a departure from the applicable framework, not merely a ratio decline.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — Classifying common-size analysis
(1, 'Analytical Procedures', 'During planning for the audit of Beacon Electronics, the auditor expresses each income statement line item as a percentage of total revenue and compares these percentages across three years. This technique is best described as:', '["Horizontal analysis comparing absolute dollar changes between periods", "Regression analysis establishing a statistical model of the relationship", "Vertical analysis using common-size financial statements to identify proportional changes", "Proof-in-total computation verifying the mathematical accuracy of recorded amounts"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Correct (C): AU-C 520.A3. Expressing each line item as a percentage of a base amount (such as revenue) creates common-size financial statements, which is a form of vertical analysis. This technique highlights proportional changes in cost structure over time. Wrong (A): Horizontal analysis compares absolute or percentage changes in the same line item across periods, not proportions relative to a base. Wrong (B): Regression analysis uses statistical modeling to establish predictive relationships, not simple percentage-of-base calculations. Wrong (D): Proof-in-total independently recalculates an expected balance; it does not express items as percentages of revenue.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: medium / L3 — Applying seasonality considerations to analytics
(1, 'Analytical Procedures', 'Canton Resorts generates 70% of its annual revenue during June through August. The auditor is performing substantive analytical procedures on quarterly revenue using prior-year quarterly data as the basis for the expectation. The auditor develops a single percentage-increase expectation applied uniformly across all four quarters. What is the primary weakness in this approach?', '["The auditor should use monthly rather than quarterly data for all analytical procedures", "Prior-year data is generally too outdated to serve as a reliable basis for expectations", "The approach fails because analytical procedures should not be applied to revenue accounts", "Applying a uniform growth rate to highly seasonal revenue ignores predictable quarterly variation and reduces the precision of the expectation"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Correct (D): AU-C 520.A12. When an entity''s operations are seasonal, the auditor must account for predictable fluctuations when developing expectations. A uniform percentage increase across quarters would understate the summer quarter expectation and overstate winter quarters, reducing the procedure''s ability to detect misstatements. Wrong (A): While more granular data improves precision, the core issue is failing to account for seasonality, not the time interval chosen. Wrong (B): Prior-year data is commonly used and generally reliable when the business environment is stable. Wrong (C): Analytical procedures can be applied to revenue accounts when relationships are sufficiently predictable.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q9: easy / L1 — Proof-in-total definition
(1, 'Analytical Procedures', 'What is a proof-in-total when used as an analytical procedure during an audit?', '["An independent calculation of an expected balance using known data inputs such as rates, quantities, or terms", "A confirmation of the total balance directly with a third party", "A complete recount of all physical items in an account", "A comparison of total assets to total liabilities on the balance sheet"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Correct (A): AU-C 520.A4. A proof-in-total is a substantive analytical procedure in which the auditor independently calculates the expected amount using reliable underlying data, such as multiplying average debt by the interest rate to estimate interest expense. It provides a high level of precision. Wrong (B): Confirmations are tests of details involving third-party corroboration, not analytical procedures. Wrong (C): Physical recounting is a test of details through observation and inspection, not an analytical procedure. Wrong (D): Comparing total assets to liabilities is a basic ratio, not a proof-in-total calculation.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q10: easy / L1 — Cross-sectional vs. time-series analysis
(1, 'Analytical Procedures', 'What is cross-sectional analysis in the context of audit analytical procedures?', '["Comparing the current year financial data to the entity''s own data from prior periods", "Comparing the entity''s financial data to industry averages or peer company data for the same period", "Analyzing the mathematical relationships within a single financial statement", "Reviewing journal entries posted across multiple accounting periods"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct (B): AU-C 520.A3. Cross-sectional analysis compares an entity''s data to external benchmarks such as industry averages or comparable companies for the same time period, helping identify anomalies relative to peers. Wrong (A): Comparing to the entity''s own prior periods is time-series or trend analysis, not cross-sectional. Wrong (C): Analyzing relationships within one statement describes vertical analysis or ratio analysis, not cross-sectional comparison. Wrong (D): Reviewing journal entries across periods is a test of details, not an analytical procedure classification.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q11: medium / L3 — Applying analytics to detect management override
(1, 'Analytical Procedures', 'While performing final analytical procedures for Delta Logistics, the auditor observes that fourth-quarter revenue is 45% higher than each of the first three quarters, yet the entity''s shipping volume data from its logistics system shows no corresponding increase. Which fraud risk does this analytical finding most directly suggest?', '["Misappropriation of assets through fictitious vendor payments to a related party", "Failure to properly capitalize fixed assets acquired during the fourth quarter", "Revenue overstatement through fictitious transactions or improper cutoff indicating management override", "An unrecorded contingent liability from pending litigation against the entity"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Correct (C): AU-C 520.07 and AU-C 240.32. When financial data diverges sharply from corroborating nonfinancial data such as shipping volume, it suggests revenue may have been fabricated or pulled forward. This pattern is a classic indicator of management override of controls through fictitious entries. Wrong (A): Asset misappropriation typically affects expense or asset accounts, not the revenue-to-volume relationship. Wrong (B): Fixed asset capitalization issues would affect the balance sheet, not the revenue-volume discrepancy pattern. Wrong (D): Contingent liabilities relate to unrecorded obligations, not revenue overstatement.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q12: medium / L2 — Using subsequent events to corroborate analytics
(1, 'Analytical Procedures', 'An auditor''s substantive analytical procedure on the allowance for doubtful accounts produces an expectation of 4.2% of receivables. Management recorded 3.1%. Before concluding, the auditor examines post-year-end cash collections and write-offs. The auditor is using this subsequent events information primarily to:', '["Replace the need for analytical procedures with direct evidence", "Test internal controls over the cash receipts cycle", "Determine the appropriate sample size for receivable confirmations", "Corroborate or contradict the analytical procedure finding by providing additional evidence about the year-end balance"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Correct (D): AU-C 520.07 and AU-C 560.11. Reviewing subsequent cash collections and write-offs provides additional evidence about the reasonableness of the year-end allowance estimate. This information helps the auditor evaluate whether the 1.1% difference identified by the analytical procedure reflects a real misstatement. Wrong (A): Subsequent events review supplements but does not replace the analytical procedure; both contribute to the evidence base. Wrong (B): Reviewing collections for estimation purposes is a substantive procedure, not a test of controls. Wrong (C): Sample size determination is made during audit planning, not during evaluation of analytical procedure results.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q13: easy / L1 — Documentation requirements for analytical procedures
(1, 'Analytical Procedures', 'What must the auditor document when performing substantive analytical procedures?', '["The expectation developed, the threshold for investigation, and the results of any investigation of significant differences", "Only the final conclusion and the name of the partner who approved the procedure", "The names of all client personnel who provided data for the analysis", "A list of all analytical procedures the auditor considered but decided not to perform"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Correct (A): AU-C 520.06. The auditor must document the expectation and factors considered in its development, the threshold for acceptable difference, and the evaluation and investigation of any differences that exceed that threshold. This creates a complete audit trail for the analytical procedure. Wrong (B): Documentation requirements extend far beyond a conclusion and partner name; the basis for the expectation and investigation results are required. Wrong (C): While data sources should be noted, the standard requires documenting the expectation, threshold, and investigation, not merely personnel names. Wrong (D): The standard requires documentation of procedures performed, not procedures considered but rejected.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q14: hard / L4 — Evaluating precision trade-offs across analytical methods
(1, 'Analytical Procedures', 'An auditor is designing substantive analytical procedures for Meridian Health Systems'' payroll expense. The entity has 2,400 employees across three pay grades, stable headcount, and predictable annual raises. The auditor must choose between three approaches: (1) compare total payroll to prior year with a 5% growth expectation, (2) multiply average headcount by average salary, or (3) calculate expected payroll by pay grade using grade-specific headcount and salary data. Which evaluation is most appropriate?', '["All three approaches provide equivalent assurance because they use the same underlying payroll data", "Approach 3 provides the most precise expectation because grade-level disaggregation uses more specific data, which may justify reduced testing of details in this area", "Approach 1 is preferable because its simplicity reduces the risk of auditor error in the calculation", "Approach 2 provides the highest assurance because average values eliminate outlier effects"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct (B): AU-C 520.A5 and .A13. Greater disaggregation and use of more specific, reliable data inputs increases the precision of the expectation and the assurance provided. Approach 3, using pay-grade-specific data, yields the most precise expectation, potentially reducing the need for supplemental tests of details on payroll. Wrong (A): The approaches differ significantly in precision; disaggregated calculations using grade-specific data provide substantially more assurance than simple prior-year comparisons. Wrong (C): Simplicity does not equate to effectiveness; Approach 1 has the lowest precision and provides the least assurance. Wrong (D): Averaging across all grades masks variation, which reduces rather than increases precision.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q15: hard / L4 — Judging when analytical procedures are insufficient as sole evidence
(1, 'Analytical Procedures', 'An auditor is determining whether substantive analytical procedures alone can provide sufficient evidence for accounts payable completeness at a manufacturing client. Accounts payable has hundreds of vendors, significant variation in purchase volumes, and several non-routine year-end accruals. The prior-year audit found two material unrecorded liabilities. Which judgment is most appropriate?', '["Substantive analytical procedures alone are sufficient if the auditor uses a highly disaggregated expectation model", "Analytical procedures should be the primary evidence source because they are more efficient than tests of details for payable balances", "Substantive analytical procedures alone are insufficient given the unpredictable relationships, prior misstatements, and significant risk; tests of details are also required", "The auditor should rely on tests of controls rather than either analytical procedures or tests of details"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Correct (C): AU-C 520.A5 and AU-C 330.21. Substantive analytical procedures are most effective when relationships are predictable and stable. Here, vendor variability, non-routine accruals, and a history of material misstatements indicate that the data relationships are insufficiently predictable. AU-C 330.21 also requires tests of details when a significant risk exists for an assertion. Wrong (A): No level of disaggregation overcomes the fundamental unpredictability and the significant risk requiring detail testing. Wrong (B): Efficiency does not override the requirement for sufficient appropriate evidence; the conditions here demand tests of details. Wrong (D): Tests of controls address operating effectiveness of controls, not the completeness assertion for the balance itself.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q16: medium / L3 — Setting investigation threshold relative to materiality
(1, 'Analytical Procedures', 'Oak Ridge Financial has overall materiality set at $500,000 and performance materiality at $375,000. The auditor is designing a substantive analytical procedure for rent expense. The auditor sets the investigation threshold at $600,000. What is the primary concern with this threshold?', '["The threshold should equal the total audit fee to maintain independence", "The threshold should be based on the prior-year rent expense, not a fixed amount", "The investigation threshold must not be set above materiality since it could allow material misstatements to go uninvestigated", "A threshold exceeding $500,000 is acceptable because substantive analytical procedures are only preliminary evidence"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Correct (C): AU-C 520.A14. The investigation threshold must be set at an amount that would identify misstatements that, individually or in aggregate, could be material. Setting the threshold at $600,000 above both overall materiality ($500,000) and performance materiality ($375,000) means misstatements material to the financial statements could go uninvestigated. Wrong (A): The investigation threshold has no relationship to audit fees; it is based on materiality considerations. Wrong (B): While prior-year data may inform the expectation, the threshold is set relative to materiality, not the prior-year balance. Wrong (D): Substantive analytical procedures can serve as primary evidence; their thresholds must still be below materiality.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q17: medium / L2 — Identifying per-unit analysis as an analytical technique
(1, 'Analytical Procedures', 'During the audit of Lakeside Hotels, the auditor calculates revenue per available room night (RevPAR) for each quarter and compares it to industry data and the entity''s prior years. This analytical procedure is best classified as:', '["A per-unit analysis using both financial and nonfinancial data to develop a precise expectation", "A proof-in-total calculation of the entity''s total revenue", "A test of controls over the revenue recognition process", "A completeness test tracing room bookings to recorded revenue"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Correct (A): AU-C 520.A3. Per-unit analysis divides a financial measure by a relevant operational metric to produce a ratio that is compared across periods or to external benchmarks. RevPAR combines revenue (financial) with available room nights (nonfinancial) for a precise, industry-standard analytical metric. Wrong (B): A proof-in-total would independently calculate total expected revenue, not a per-unit ratio. Wrong (C): RevPAR analysis is a substantive analytical procedure, not a test of controls. Wrong (D): Tracing bookings to revenue is a test of details for completeness, not an analytical procedure.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q18: easy / L2 — Classifying predictability of accounts for analytics
(1, 'Analytical Procedures', 'An auditor is deciding which accounts are most suitable for substantive analytical procedures. Which of the following accounts generally has the most predictable relationships, making it best suited for substantive analytical procedures?', '["Accounts receivable, which depends on individual customer payment behavior", "Depreciation expense, which can be independently calculated from known asset data, useful lives, and methods", "Goodwill, which is based on fair value judgments from past acquisitions", "Contingent liabilities, which depend on uncertain future legal outcomes"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct (B): AU-C 520.A5. Depreciation expense has highly predictable relationships because it is based on known inputs such as asset cost, useful life, and depreciation method. This makes it one of the most suitable accounts for substantive analytical procedures. Wrong (A): Receivable balances depend on individual customer behavior, making relationships less predictable for analytical procedures. Wrong (C): Goodwill involves significant judgment in fair value measurements, reducing predictability. Wrong (D): Contingent liabilities are inherently uncertain and unpredictable, making them poorly suited for analytical procedures.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q19: medium / L3 — Applying analytics with multiple independent variables
(1, 'Analytical Procedures', 'The auditor of Pacific Energy Corp is developing an expectation for electricity revenue. The auditor identifies three key drivers: customer count (increased 3%), average rate per kWh (increased 6% due to regulatory approval), and average consumption per customer (decreased 2% due to mild weather). Rather than using a simple prior-year comparison, the auditor builds an expectation incorporating all three variables. This approach most directly improves the:', '["Timeliness of the audit evidence by completing the procedure earlier", "Objectivity of the auditor by reducing management involvement", "Precision of the expectation by incorporating multiple independent data sources that explain the expected change", "Sufficiency of evidence by replacing the need for any tests of details on revenue"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Correct (C): AU-C 520.A5. Using multiple independent variables such as customer count, rate changes, and consumption data to build the expectation increases precision because each variable explains a specific component of the revenue change. Higher precision provides greater assurance from the analytical procedure. Wrong (A): Using more variables improves precision, not timeliness; the procedure may actually take longer. Wrong (B): Incorporating operational data improves precision, not auditor objectivity, which relates to independence and bias. Wrong (D): Even highly precise analytical procedures do not automatically eliminate the need for all tests of details.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q20: easy / L1 — Purpose of analytical procedures in review engagements
(1, 'Analytical Procedures', 'In a review engagement performed under SSARS, analytical procedures serve what primary purpose?', '["To detect and correct all material misstatements in the financial statements", "To test the operating effectiveness of the entity''s internal controls", "To provide a basis for expressing an audit opinion on the financial statements", "To provide a basis for obtaining limited assurance that no material modifications are needed"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Correct (D): AR-C 90.20. In a review engagement, analytical procedures and inquiries are the primary procedures used to obtain limited assurance. The accountant''s conclusion states whether any material modifications should be made to the financial statements. Wrong (A): A review provides limited, not reasonable, assurance and is not designed to detect all material misstatements. Wrong (B): Testing internal controls is an audit procedure, not part of a review engagement. Wrong (C): An audit opinion requires reasonable assurance; a review engagement provides only limited assurance through a conclusion.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q21: easy / L1 — Definition of horizontal analysis
(1, 'Analytical Procedures', 'What does horizontal analysis involve when used as an analytical procedure?', '["Comparing the same financial statement line items across two or more periods to identify trends and changes", "Expressing each line item as a percentage of a base amount within a single period", "Computing statistical correlations between financial and nonfinancial variables", "Verifying the mathematical accuracy of the financial statement totals"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Correct (A): AU-C 520.A3. Horizontal analysis compares the same accounts across multiple periods, identifying dollar and percentage changes over time. It is a fundamental trend analysis technique used in planning and final analytical procedures. Wrong (B): Expressing items as percentages of a base within one period describes vertical or common-size analysis. Wrong (C): Statistical correlation analysis is a more advanced technique such as regression, not basic horizontal analysis. Wrong (D): Verifying mathematical accuracy is a recalculation procedure, not horizontal analysis.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q22: medium / L2 — Identifying the effect of entity complexity on analytical precision
(1, 'Analytical Procedures', 'An auditor is comparing the effectiveness of substantive analytical procedures for two clients: Client A is a single-product company with stable operations, and Client B is a diversified conglomerate with five unrelated business segments. The auditor applies the same consolidated-level analytical procedure to both. The procedure is likely to be:', '["Equally effective for both clients because the same technique is used", "More effective for Client A because the single-product operations create more predictable financial relationships at the consolidated level", "Less effective for Client A because smaller entities have less reliable data", "More effective for Client B because diversification stabilizes aggregate financial results"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct (B): AU-C 520.A13. Analytical procedures are most effective when financial relationships are predictable. A single-product company with stable operations has more predictable consolidated relationships than a diversified conglomerate where segment-level variations can offset each other and mask anomalies. Wrong (A): Effectiveness depends on the nature of the entity''s operations, not merely the technique applied. Wrong (C): Entity size and data reliability are separate concepts; single-product companies often have more predictable relationships. Wrong (D): Diversification may stabilize aggregate results, but this actually reduces the analytical procedure''s ability to detect segment-level misstatements.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q23: medium / L2 — Identifying appropriate data sources for expectations
(1, 'Analytical Procedures', 'An auditor is developing an expectation for a client''s natural gas utility revenue. Which combination of data sources would produce the most reliable expectation for the substantive analytical procedure?', '["Management''s internal revenue forecast and the prior-year recorded revenue", "The client''s marketing department projections and press releases", "Regulatory-approved rate schedules from the public utility commission combined with independently verified customer count and consumption data", "The client''s board minutes discussing expected revenue targets"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Correct (C): AU-C 520.A5. The most reliable data for developing expectations comes from sources independent of the entity or that are subject to external verification. Regulatory-approved rates are publicly set, and independently verified consumption data is more reliable than management-generated forecasts. Wrong (A): Management''s internal forecast is generated by the entity and subject to potential bias, reducing reliability. Wrong (B): Marketing projections and press releases are management-generated and forward-looking, not independently verifiable. Wrong (D): Board minutes reflect management''s expectations, not independently verifiable data.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q24: hard / L3 — Applying analytics to a complex estimation scenario
(1, 'Analytical Procedures', 'Sterling Insurance Co. reported a loss reserve of $42 million. The auditor develops an independent expectation using paid-loss development factors and industry loss triangles, arriving at $48.5 million. The investigation threshold is $3 million. Management explains the $6.5 million difference is due to a proprietary actuarial model incorporating favorable claim trends not reflected in industry data. The auditor has no access to the proprietary model details. What is the most appropriate response?', '["Accept management''s explanation because actuarial estimates are inherently uncertain", "Withdraw from the engagement because access to the model was denied", "Reduce the investigation threshold to narrow the difference", "Obtain corroborating evidence for the favorable trends, consider engaging an auditor''s specialist, and evaluate whether the difference indicates potential management bias"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Correct (D): AU-C 520.07 and AU-C 540.13. When a significant difference exceeds the threshold and management''s explanation relies on unverifiable proprietary data, the auditor cannot simply accept the explanation. The auditor should seek corroborating evidence, consider using a specialist per AU-C 620, and evaluate whether the consistently favorable direction suggests management bias. Wrong (A): Inherent uncertainty does not relieve the auditor of investigating a $6.5 million difference that exceeds the threshold. Wrong (B): Withdrawal is premature; the auditor should first attempt to obtain evidence through alternative means including a specialist. Wrong (C): Adjusting the threshold after the fact to accommodate the difference is inappropriate and undermines the procedure''s objectivity.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q25: medium / L2 — Identifying when prior audit findings affect current analytics
(1, 'Analytical Procedures', 'During planning analytics for Novus Corp, the auditor notes that the prior-year audit identified a $200,000 overstatement in prepaid insurance that was corrected by an audit adjustment. In the current year, the auditor is developing an expectation for prepaid insurance. How should the prior-year finding influence the current analytical procedure?', '["The auditor should use the corrected prior-year balance, not the originally recorded amount, as the baseline for the current-year expectation", "The finding is irrelevant because each year''s analytical procedures are independent", "The auditor should add $200,000 to the current expectation to compensate for the prior-year error", "The auditor should use the originally recorded amount because it reflects actual accounting processes"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Correct (A): AU-C 520.A5. The expectation should be based on reliable, corrected data. Using the unadjusted prior-year balance would incorporate a known misstatement into the baseline, reducing the precision and reliability of the current-year analytical procedure. Wrong (B): Prior-year findings are relevant context; a recurring misstatement in the same account indicates elevated risk. Wrong (C): Adding the prior error amount to the expectation is incorrect; the corrected balance is the appropriate starting point. Wrong (D): The originally recorded amount contained a known misstatement and is not reliable for developing expectations.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q26: medium / L2 — Role of professional skepticism in evaluating management explanations
(1, 'Analytical Procedures', 'After identifying a significant unexpected increase in the client''s warranty reserve through analytical procedures, the auditor inquires of management. Management provides a plausible explanation citing new product line risk. The auditor should next:', '["Accept the explanation and document it without further action", "Obtain corroborating evidence supporting management''s explanation, such as warranty claim data for the new product line", "Disregard management''s explanation entirely because it is not independent evidence", "Immediately propose an audit adjustment to reduce the reserve to the expected amount"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct (B): AU-C 520.07 and AU-C 200.17. Professional skepticism requires that the auditor not accept management explanations at face value. The auditor should corroborate the explanation with supporting evidence, such as reviewing warranty claims data, product return rates, or engineering reports related to the new product line. Wrong (A): Accepting without corroboration fails to maintain professional skepticism as required by auditing standards. Wrong (C): Management''s explanation provides useful context and should be considered, but it must be corroborated with independent evidence. Wrong (D): Proposing an adjustment is premature before determining whether the recorded amount or the auditor''s expectation is more accurate.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q27: hard / L4 — Evaluating the overall sufficiency of analytical procedures as evidence
(1, 'Analytical Procedures', 'An auditor is completing the audit of Summit Aerospace and has used substantive analytical procedures as the primary evidence for five income statement accounts: depreciation, rent, interest expense, payroll, and utilities. For each account, the expectation was within the investigation threshold. No tests of details were performed on any of these accounts. Assessed control risk is moderate for all five. Which evaluation of this approach is most appropriate?', '["The approach is unacceptable because analytical procedures can never serve as primary evidence for any income statement account", "The approach is acceptable for all five accounts because each difference was within the investigation threshold established during planning", "The approach may be acceptable for depreciation, rent, and interest, but the auditor must evaluate whether payroll and utilities have sufficiently predictable relationships for analytics alone", "The approach is acceptable only if the engagement partner provides specific written approval for each account tested analytically"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Correct (C): AU-C 520.A5 and AU-C 330.18. While substantive analytical procedures can serve as the primary evidence source for accounts with predictable relationships, the auditor must evaluate whether each specific account meets this criterion. Depreciation, rent, and interest typically have highly predictable inputs; payroll and utilities may require additional evaluation. The auditor must also consider whether aggregate reliance on analytics across five accounts provides sufficient evidence overall. Wrong (A): Standards permit substantive analytical procedures as primary evidence when conditions are met; a blanket prohibition is incorrect. Wrong (B): Simply being within threshold is necessary but not sufficient; the auditor must also evaluate the suitability of analytical procedures for each account. Wrong (D): Partner approval is not a substitute for evaluating the appropriateness and sufficiency of the procedures themselves.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q28: medium / L2 — Identifying break-even analysis as an analytical tool
(1, 'Analytical Procedures', 'An auditor calculates a client''s break-even point in units and compares it to actual sales volume to evaluate the reasonableness of reported operating income. The auditor is using this analytical technique primarily to:', '["Test the operating effectiveness of controls over the revenue cycle", "Determine the appropriate audit fee for the engagement", "Verify that all sales transactions were recorded in the correct period", "Assess whether the reported profit level is consistent with the entity''s cost structure and sales volume"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Correct (D): AU-C 520.A3. Break-even analysis relates fixed costs, variable costs, and sales volume to expected profitability. By comparing actual volume to the break-even point, the auditor can assess whether the reported operating income is consistent with the entity''s economic fundamentals, providing evidence about the plausibility of reported results. Wrong (A): Break-even analysis is a substantive analytical procedure, not a test of controls. Wrong (B): Audit fee determination is an engagement management decision unrelated to analytical procedures. Wrong (C): Break-even analysis evaluates overall profitability relationships, not cutoff accuracy of individual transactions.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q29: easy / L1 — Who develops the independent expectation
(1, 'Analytical Procedures', 'Who is responsible for developing the independent expectation used in a substantive analytical procedure?', '["The auditor performing the procedure", "The client''s management or accounting department", "The audit committee of the board of directors", "An external industry analyst or consultant"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Correct (A): AU-C 520.05. The auditor is responsible for developing an independent expectation of the recorded amount when performing a substantive analytical procedure. The expectation must be developed using reliable data and reflect the auditor''s own analysis. Wrong (B): Management provides data the auditor may use, but the expectation must be independently developed by the auditor. Wrong (C): The audit committee provides governance oversight but does not develop audit expectations. Wrong (D): External analysts may provide reference data, but the auditor must independently develop and evaluate the expectation.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q30: easy / L1 — Analytical procedures and detection risk
(1, 'Analytical Procedures', 'In the audit risk model, what is the primary effect of using a more precise substantive analytical procedure?', '["It increases inherent risk for the related account", "It reduces detection risk by providing more persuasive substantive evidence", "It eliminates the need to assess control risk", "It automatically lowers materiality for the financial statements as a whole"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Correct (B): AU-C 520.A5 and AU-C 200.A42. A more precise analytical procedure provides more persuasive evidence, which reduces detection risk, the risk that the auditor''s procedures fail to detect a material misstatement. Lower detection risk contributes to achieving an acceptably low level of audit risk. Wrong (A): Inherent risk is a characteristic of the entity and its transactions, not affected by the auditor''s procedures. Wrong (C): Control risk assessment is based on the design and operating effectiveness of controls, independent of substantive procedures. Wrong (D): Materiality is set based on user needs and the financial statements as a whole, not driven by the precision of individual procedures.', 'easy', 1)
;

COMMIT;
